10:01:01 said  I checked the older lead/rhythm splitter against your spec. All four serious problems you listed were still there, and I fixed them:
10:01:01 said  - **Scoring:** it now decides mostly from the playing itself (single notes vs chords, whether the notes change on each attack, how many pitches sound at once). Register, sustain and the transcriber's own confidence count for at most 10% together.
10:01:01 said  - **Pitch detection:** the old check called a power chord a "confident single note"; it no longer does.
10:01:01 said  - **When unsure:** a tied score, or two parts that differ only in register or tone, now give "ambiguous" and no separate guitar files, even in best-effort mode. Empty or very short tracks are skipped instead of guessed at.
10:01:01 said  - **Diagnostics:** every threshold and weight is labelled as not yet tuned to real songs and listed in the diagnostics file.
10:01:01 said  Your spec's test cases are now automated and pass, along with the rest of the tests, and the project check passes. That includes a strum panned left with a melody panned right, where the melody is correctly picked as lead.
10:01:01 said  Not done yet:
10:01:01 said  - The older splitter still judges two long stretches of the song, not 8-bar sections that can switch roles.
10:01:01 said  - The other proposed measures (chord fit, bends, beat lock, stereo layout, how loud the guitar is in the full mix) aren't built.
10:01:01 said  - Nothing has been tuned on real songs.
10:01:01 said  The best next step is to hand-label 30–50 short sections of your own songs as lead, rhythm or unclear, so the error rate can be measured and the numbers tuned.
